>>
>>How about this for an anti-pudding farming measure? Puddings recombine.
>>Two neighbouring puddings have a chance of combining back into one
>>bigger pudding. You could even make it so the the combined pudding was
>>stronger than before. That would make farming a much riskier business.
>>
>>Personally, though, I still think that pudding farming is interesting
>>behaviour for the game, and shouldn't be penalised. At worst, we could
>>remove death-drops for puddings, so that they only ever leave corpses.
> 
> 
> I'd like to see death-drops eliminated altogether.  Instead, create the
> item when the monster is generated and put it in its inventory.  It
> seems silly for a dwarf to drop a nice offensive wand that it couldn't
> use because it wasn't created until the dwarf died.

No it isn't. It is the same effect as you get when you look at your 
inventory after death and notice the scroll of teleportation that might 
have saved you.

The main reason of death drop is to introduce a constant flow of new 
items that haven't had a chance on monster using them. If we eliminate 
death drop then it would mean that after you've cleared the items that 
were created during the level creation almost no more of usable one shot 
items would be available as most of them would be used by the monsters 
before you have any chance getting your hands on them.

If we think death drop as being the item that the intelligent monster 
just forgot having then the only problem is: why didn't my probing show it?

My suggestion is to eliminate the item creation at the death of the 
monster and move it to the creation of the monster, but label the item 
as unusable by the owner. As it still is in the monsters inventory 
probing will work fine. Dividing or cloning a monster wouldn't create 
new items but distribute the existing inventory items between the old 
and the new monster; the unusability flag would remain. Death of the 
monster would clear the flag so that other monsters can have a chance on 
picking the item up and using it.

